Thought: The task is to determine the SMILES strings for the connected fragments of a compound with the molecular formula C9H13NO2. The inputs suggest that the compound may have a bromine atom and a certain number of carbon, hydrogen, nitrogen, and oxygen atoms. To identify the fragments, I need to understand the structure of the compound. Using NMR and mass spectrometry data can help elucidate the structure. I will start by obtaining the HSQC NMR spectra to gather information about the carbon-hydrogen framework of the compound.
Action: hsqc_nmr_spectra
Action Input: {}

Thought: The HSQC NMR spectra provide information about the hydrogen and carbon chemical shifts, which can help identify the types of fragments present in the compound. The data suggests the presence of aromatic protons (7.17/129 and 7.93/129.7) and aliphatic protons (2.58/38.6, 2.84/43.5, and 3.81/52.2). The presence of aromatic protons indicates an aromatic ring, likely a benzene ring. The aliphatic protons suggest the presence of alkyl chains or other functional groups. To further confirm the structure, I will retrieve the carbon chemical shifts to identify functional groups.
Action: retrieve_carbon_shifts
Action Input: {}

Thought: The carbon chemical shifts provide further insight into the functional groups present in the compound. The shifts at 38.6 ppm and 43.5 ppm suggest the presence of alkyl groups (RCH₂ or R₂CH). The shift at 52.2 ppm is indicative of a methoxy group (H₃C-O). The aromatic carbon shifts at 129 ppm and 129.7 ppm confirm the presence of an aromatic ring, likely a benzene ring. Given the presence of a bromine atom, it is possible that the compound is a bromo-substituted aromatic compound with additional alkyl and methoxy groups. To further confirm the structure, I will retrieve the proton chemical shifts to identify the types of protons present.
Action: retrieve_protons_shifts
